rname{sin}⁡(4y+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${p}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={p}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{p}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)} \\\\ {({p}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/state.json" "file_sha256" "92636fd31d02c0dd27ff45c17964c0bb650eeb8093fdc371c1a377598fe9ac8d"} {"question" "q-73074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2e156de1335d27406e8b355259b19f7acc6c7f4ff91a253871efe5a495eeb82e"} {"question" "q-73074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/state.json" "file_sha256" "92636fd31d02c0dd27ff45c17964c0bb650eeb8093fdc371c1a377598fe9ac8d"} {"question" "q-73074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4)}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x))}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2{p}^{-1}(x)+1)}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/state.json" "file_sha256" "92636fd31d02c0dd27ff45c17964c0bb650eeb8093fdc371c1a377598fe9ac8d"} {"question" "q-73074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({p}^{-1}(x))-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{p}^{-1}(x)+2)}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/state.json" "file_sha256" "92636fd31d02c0dd27ff45c17964c0bb650eeb8093fdc371c1a377598fe9ac8d"} {"question" "e-196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)=3x^2+9,$ find $(f^{-1})'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/example-196.json" "file_sha256" "5a5aac4e3212f749841f741122b2fa530b761c0e14b62a771111542ebb6516eb"} {"question" "e-196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y = 3x^2+9,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3y^2+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y=f^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\dfrac{ \\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(3{y}^{2}+9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =6y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{6y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$ Since $y=f^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{6{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ ({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{6{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/example-196.json" "file_sha256" "5a5aac4e3212f749841f741122b2fa530b761c0e14b62a771111542ebb6516eb"} {"question" "e-197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x)$ if $f(x) = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/example-197.json" "file_sha256" "5ead5f265841e14a358aa63942e03396e99b5ca4b322fa0dc0b6fb2fe9a2beed"} {"question" "e-197" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos \\left(y^2\\right) + 6\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y=f^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\dfrac{ \\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})+6] \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2y}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x).$ Since $y=f^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{1}{2({f}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}) \\\\ ({f}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2({f}^{-1}(x))}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({({f}^{-1}(x))}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979277/example-197.json" "file_sha256" "5ead5f265841e14a358aa63942e03396e99b5ca4b322fa0dc0b6fb2fe9a2beed"}] "usage" {"e-197" [] "e-196" []} "basis" 846 "retractions" [[17592186119462 :question/problem "If $f(x)=3x^2+9$, find $(f^{-1})'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x)$."] [17592186119462 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is\n\n$$\ny = 3x^2+9\n$$\n\nand to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=3y^2+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}\n$$\n\nbut the equation is already solved for $x$, so we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nand then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}(3y^{2} + 9) \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= 6y \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{6y}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x)$. Since\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}[f^{-1}(x)] &= (1)/(6f^{-1}(x)) \\\\\n(f^{-1})^{′}(x) &= (1)/(6f^{-1}(x))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119463 :question/problem "Find $\\left(f^{-1}\\right)'(x)$ as a function of $f^{-1}(x)$ if $f(x) = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6$."] [17592186119463 :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is\n\n$$\ny = \\cos \\left(x^2\\right) +6\n$$\n\nand to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos \\left(y^2\\right) + 6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx}\n$$\n\nbut the equation is already solved for $x$, so we compute\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy}\n$$\n\nand then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= \\frac{d}{dy}[\\cos (y^{2}) + 6] \\\\\n\\frac{dx}{dy} &= -2y\\sin (y^{2}) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= - (1)/(2y\\sin (y^{2})) \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= -\\frac{1}{2y}\\csc (y^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of $f^{-1}(x)$. Since\n\n$$\ny=f^{-1}(x)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}[f^{-1}(x)] &= - (1)/(2(f^{-1}(x)))\\csc ((f^{-1}(x))^{2}) \\\\\n(f^{-1})^{′}(x) &= - (1)/(2(f^{-1}(x)))\\csc ((f^{-1}(x))^{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
